= Work by Hugh W. Catts
{parent=Hugh W. Catts}
{c}

= Language disorder
{parent=Clinical psychology}

A pattern of <language> difficulties associated with persistent functional impact. A <language> difference related to experience or multilingual context is not by itself a disorder.

= Specific language impairment
{parent=Language disorder}

= SLI
{synonym}
{c}

A historical classification used with differing inclusion and exclusion criteria. It should not be assumed identical to every current <Developmental language disorder>[DLD] definition when comparing studies.

= CATALISE
{parent=Language disorder}
{c}

A multinational Delphi project developing consensus on identification and terminology for developmental <language> difficulties. Its terminology agreement should be distinguished from an experiment establishing a natural diagnostic boundary.<Phase 2 of CATALISE: a multinational and multidisciplinary Delphi consensus study of problems with language development: Terminology by Dorothy V. M. Bishop and others>[Journal of Child Psychology and Psychiatry 58, 1068–1080; 57-member terminology consensus, impairment, prognosis and biomedical associations]{ref}

= Psychological trauma
{parent=Clinical psychology}

An experience with potentially severe psychological consequences, interpreted within the person's circumstances and available support. <Psychological trauma>[Trauma] <Exposure therapy>[exposure] does not imply that everyone develops a persistent disorder.

= Mechanism of psychological treatment
{parent=Clinical psychology}

A process through which an intervention produces an outcome. Temporal <Statistical mediation>[mediation], experimental target manipulation and active treatment comparisons supply different constraints; a successful package does not prove its complete explanatory theory.

= Clinical supervision
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Structured review supporting clinical reasoning, competence and treatment delivery. The content of supervision can matter when an <outcome-feedback> intervention depends on clinicians responding to an alert.

= Psychotherapy
{parent=Clinical psychology}

= Psychological treatment
{synonym}

Clinical intervention using psychological methods to address <distress>, impairment or a specified psychological difficulty. Treatment packages can include several techniques; demonstrating benefit from the package does not establish the necessity of each.

= Virtual reality therapy
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Use of a computer-generated interactive environment in <psychological treatment> or experiments. Head tracking and a head-mounted display can standardise social encounters, while real-world transfer, accessibility and tolerability remain empirical questions.

= Virtual-reality delusion experiment
{parent=Virtual reality therapy}

Freeman and colleagues compared graded virtual social <Exposure therapy>[exposure] with threat testing that included <safety-behavior> reduction, assessing <belief> conviction and later real-world <distress>. The brief small-sample experiment probes a combined causal manipulation rather than establishing durable effects of every component.<Virtual reality in the treatment of persecutory delusions: randomised controlled experimental study testing how to reduce delusional conviction by Daniel Freeman and others>[British Journal of Psychiatry 209, 62–67; 30 participants, graded social environments, safety reduction versus exposure and real-world assessment]{ref}

= Contributory-causal treatment development
{parent=Clinical psychology}

A research approach that identifies a potentially modifiable maintaining process, changes it experimentally and tests consequences for a clinical difficulty. Changing one contributory cause need not eliminate the difficulty, and a multicomponent manipulation does not identify one exclusive pathway.<Effects of cognitive behaviour therapy for worry on persecutory delusions in patients with psychosis (WIT): a parallel, single-blind, randomised controlled trial with a mediation analysis by Daniel Freeman and others>[Lancet Psychiatry 2, 305–313; 150 patients, six worry sessions plus usual care versus usual care, eight-/24-week outcomes]{ref}

= OASIS insomnia trial
{parent=Contributory-causal treatment development}
{c}

A trial of digital <CBT> for insomnia versus usual practice in university students with insomnia. Changes in psychotic experiences support investigation of sleep as a contributory process, but the population and comparator constrain application to clinical <delusions>.<The effects of improving sleep on mental health (OASIS): a randomised controlled trial with mediation analysis by Daniel Freeman and others>[Lancet Psychiatry 4, 749–758; 3755 university students with insomnia, digital CBT versus usual practice and population limits]{ref}

= Worry Intervention Trial
{parent=Contributory-causal treatment development}
{c}

= WIT
{synonym}
{c}

A <Randomization>[randomised] trial of a brief worry-focused intervention added to standard care for persistent <persecutory delusions>. The standard-care comparator permits inference about the added package, while unequal therapeutic contact limits a more ingredient-specific claim.<Effects of cognitive behaviour therapy for worry on persecutory delusions in patients with psychosis (WIT): a parallel, single-blind, randomised controlled trial with a mediation analysis by Daniel Freeman and others>[Lancet Psychiatry 2, 305–313; 150 patients, six worry sessions plus usual care versus usual care, eight-/24-week outcomes]{ref}

= Multisystemic therapy
{parent=Clinical psychology}

An intensive family- and community-oriented intervention for serious adolescent behavioural difficulties. <Treatment> addresses maintaining factors across home, peers and school. Its comparative effectiveness depends on population, delivery and the services available to the control group.<Multisystemic therapy versus management as usual in the treatment of adolescent antisocial behaviour (START) by Peter Fonagy and others>[Lancet Psychiatry 5, 119–133; Methods, primary outcome and Findings at 18 months]{ref}

= Therapeutic alliance
{parent=Clinical psychology}

A collaborative treatment relationship involving shared goals, tasks and trust. A measure of reported <empathy> or agreement is not by itself an observation of all aspects of the <therapeutic alliance>.

= Psychopathology
{parent=Clinical psychology}

The study and description of mental difficulties and the processes associated with their development and persistence. A <symptom> description, a diagnostic category, an etiological explanation and a maintaining mechanism answer different questions.

= Psychopathy
{parent=Psychopathology}

A construct involving characteristic affective, interpersonal and behavioural features. Findings in a selected offender sample should not be generalized to everyone with antisocial behaviour or used to infer an individual’s moral character from a brain signal.

= Psychosis
{parent=Psychopathology}

A clinical description involving marked disturbances in perception, belief, thought or related functioning, assessed in context. <Psychosis> is not synonymous with <schizophrenia>, dangerousness or loss of all capacities.

= Hallucination
{parent=Psychosis}

A perception-like experience without a corresponding external stimulus, distinguished from a mistaken interpretation of an actual stimulus. The modality, context and impact matter for clinical interpretation.

= Aberrant salience
{parent=Psychosis}

A theoretical account in which inappropriate assignment of significance to events contributes to psychotic experience and its interpretation. It links valuation, <learning> and belief formation without making them identical.

= Salience Attribution Test
{parent=Aberrant salience}
{c}

A task using multidimensional cues for which one feature predicts <reinforcement> and another does not. Explicit ratings and response times assess adaptive and aberrant cue effects separately. An absolute rating difference also increases with measurement noise.<Do patients with schizophrenia exhibit aberrant salience? by Jonathan P. Roiser and others>[Psychological Medicine 39, 199–209; Salience Attribution Test, Results and study limitations]{ref}

= Delusion
{parent=Psychosis}

A conviction assessed in relation to the evidence, its responsiveness to alternatives and the person's cultural and clinical context. Disagreement, an unfamiliar cultural explanation or unusual content alone is not a sufficient assessment.

= Delusional conviction
{parent=Delusion}

The degree of confidence in a delusional <belief>, often measured dimensionally. A change in conviction is not automatically identical to categorical <Clinical recovery>[recovery], reduced <distress> or restored functioning.

= Persecutory delusion
{parent=Delusion}

A <belief> that others intend harm, assessed as a <delusion> in its clinical and contextual setting. Psychological formulations examine processes such as <worry>, felt vulnerability and <safety behaviors> without assuming a single cause of every presentation.

= Psychotic experience
{parent=Psychopathology}

An experience resembling phenomena assessed in <psychosis>, such as a hallucination or unusual conviction. <Psychotic experiences> can occur with or without a clinical disorder; a research definition must specify persistence, context and consequences.

= Psychosis continuum hypothesis
{parent=Psychotic experience}

The hypothesis that some <psychosis>-related experiences vary dimensionally across clinical and nonclinical populations. Overlap in a dimension does not imply that all people share the same cause, prognosis or <need for care>.

= Hoarding
{parent=Psychopathology}

Persistent retention or accumulation of possessions. In clinical research, the observable accumulation must be distinguished from the reasons for retaining objects, difficulty discarding, environmental constraints and associated impairment.

= Attachment to possessions
{parent=Hoarding}

Emotional significance assigned to objects, including their relation to memory, identity or anticipated use. A research account must distinguish attachment from inability to categorize or act on a decision.

= Discarding difficulty
{parent=Hoarding}

Difficulty making or carrying out decisions to relinquish possessions. Measuring <discarding difficulty> separately from clutter helps distinguish a proposed process from an outcome also influenced by space, resources and other household members.

= Symptom equivalence does not establish mechanistic identity
{parent=Psychopathology}

Let $Z$ index either of two candidate pathways and suppose $P(Y=1\mid Z=1)=P(Y=1\mid Z=2)=p$ for a measured <symptom> $Y$. For any mixing <probability> $\pi=P(Z=1)$, marginalization gives $P(Y=1)=\pi p+(1-\pi)p=p$. An independent sample with $k$ positive observations out of $n$ therefore has <likelihood> $p^k(1-p)^{n-k}$, independent of $\pi$. The <symptom> alone does not identify the mixture proportion or pathway. This is an <identifiability> counterexample, not an empirical claim that real clinical pathways necessarily have identical <symptom> distributions. Additional indicators or <interventions> may distinguish them.

= Equifinality
{parent=Psychopathology}

Convergence of different starting conditions or processes on a similar outcome. <Equifinality> cautions against inferring a unique mechanism from a <symptom> alone; it does not show that a syndrome-level classification is useless.

= Etiology
{parent=Psychopathology}

The origins or causes of a condition. An etiological factor can differ from the process that currently maintains a difficulty, so changing a maintainer need not establish the original cause.

= Nosology
{parent=Psychopathology}

The classification of disorders. A useful <nosology> supports reliable communication and informative predictions; reliability of a category is different from validity of a proposed cause.

= Symptom
{parent=Psychopathology}

An experience or difficulty relevant to assessment. The same <symptom> can occur in different conditions or outside a disorder; interpreting it requires its wider context, course and consequences.

= Clinical syndrome
{parent=Psychopathology}

A recognizable pattern of associated experiences or signs with clinical significance. A coherent <clinical syndrome> need not have a single cause, and covariance among its indicators does not alone establish their causal organization.

= Diathesis-stress model
{parent=Clinical psychology}

An account in which vulnerability and stressful circumstances jointly influence an outcome. A <psychological stressor> can increase risk without being universally necessary or sufficient, and the vulnerability can itself have developmental and environmental causes.

= Stress sensitization
{parent=Diathesis-stress model}

A pattern in which prior <adversity> increases sensitivity to later <psychological stress>, such as a lower subsequent <psychological stress> level accompanying <depression>. Demonstrating the pattern does not by itself identify its mechanism or exclude differences in <exposure> measurement and prior liability.<Depression and sensitization to stressors among young women as a function of childhood adversity by Constance Hammen and others>[2000, sensitization hypothesis and prospective test]{ref}

= Psychological resilience
{parent=Clinical psychology}

= Resilient adaptation
{synonym}

Successful adaptation in the context of significant <adversity>, specified by outcome domain, developmental context and time. It is not simply the absence of disorder in an unexposed person, a universally favorable <personality> profile or proof of invulnerability. Capacities and resources proposed to explain adaptation should be measured separately from the outcome they are meant to explain.<The Construct of Resilience by Suniya S. Luthar and others>[2000, definition and multidimensionality]{ref}

= Stress inoculation
{parent=Psychological resilience}

A proposed protective effect of manageable prior challenges on later responses. A curved association between lifetime <adversity> and current functioning is not sufficient to establish that effect causally or identify a safe beneficial <exposure> for an individual. Human observational and animal experimental evidence answer different questions.<Whatever Does Not Kill Us by Mark D. Seery and others>[2010, nonmonotonic longitudinal associations]{ref}

= Promotive factor
{parent=Psychological resilience}

A resource associated with more favorable functioning across the <exposure> levels under study. This describes a general favorable association, distinguished here from an <exposure>-dependent <stress-buffering interaction>. Terminology varies across the literature, so a study should specify its statistical and causal meaning.

= Measurement of psychological resilience
{parent=Psychological resilience}

Measurement relating documented <adversity> to subsequent adaptation. A design specifies <exposure>, outcome, baseline functioning and follow-up; capacity <questionnaires>, trajectories and <exposure>-conditioned <regression residual> scores operationalize different questions.

= Residualized psychological resilience score
{parent=Measurement of psychological resilience}
{title2=$R_i=\widehat m(S_i)-D_i$}

An <exposure>-conditioned adaptation measure. With distress $D_i$, <exposure> $S_i$ and predicted distress $\widehat m(S_i)$, the displayed convention makes higher scores mean less distress than predicted. The result depends on <exposure> measurement, reference population and model. A <regression residual> can include <measurement error> and omitted causes rather than a distinct protective capacity.

For <ordinary least squares> $D=X\widehat\beta+e$, the <normal equation> gives $X^Te=0$. Thus $R=-e$ is also orthogonal to the fitted predictors; with an intercept its sample mean is zero. A zero in-sample linear association between <exposure> and this score can therefore be an algebraic consequence of its construction, not evidence that <exposure> has no effect. Independent validation is needed before assigning substantive meaning to the <regression residual>.

= Connor-Davidson Resilience Scale
{parent=Measurement of psychological resilience}
{c}

= CD-RISC
{c}
{synonym}

A <self-report> measure of <coping>-related qualities developed by <Kathryn M. Connor> and <Jonathan R. T. Davidson>. The original version has 25 items scored from zero to four. <Measurement reliability> and treatment-associated change concern the measured <self-reports>; they do not substitute for prospective evidence of adaptation to a documented <adversity>.<Development of a new resilience scale by Kathryn M. Connor and Jonathan R. T. Davidson>[2003, original scale and validation]{ref}

= Psychological resilience trajectory
{parent=Measurement of psychological resilience}

A pattern of functioning across time around <adversity>. Stable adaptation and <recovery after adversity> can produce similar scores at a late follow-up while reflecting different histories. Estimated trajectory classes depend on measurement and statistical assumptions; they should not automatically be treated as discrete natural types.

= Delayed psychological distress
{parent=Psychological resilience trajectory}

Increasing difficulty that becomes evident later rather than immediately after an event. Sparse follow-up or selective attrition can hide such patterns or make their apparent onset misleading.

= Recovery after adversity
{parent=Psychological resilience trajectory}

A return toward earlier or adequate functioning after disruption. <Recovery after adversity> differs from maintaining low disturbance throughout, and its evaluation depends on the baseline and observation window.

= Access to mental health care
{parent=Clinical psychology}

The practical possibility of recognising need, seeking and receiving appropriate support, and continuing to effective treatment. Referral counts, treatment starts, treatment completion and clinical benefit are different stages. Equity assessment must include eligible people who never enter or discontinue care.<Barriers and facilitators to parents seeking and accessing professional support for anxiety disorders in children: qualitative interview study by Tessa Reardon and others>[European Child and Adolescent Psychiatry 27, 1023–1031; interviews with parents of sixteen school-identified children aged 7–11]{ref}

= Improving Access to Psychological Therapies
{parent=Access to mental health care}
{c}

= IAPT
{synonym}
{c}

The historical English programme expanding evidence-based psychological care for <depression> and <anxiety>. Sessional outcome measurement made service results unusually complete and publicly inspectable. The clinical effects of particular feedback arrangements should be distinguished from the feasibility and transparency of collecting data.<Transparency about the outcomes of mental health services (IAPT approach): an analysis of public data by David M. Clark and others>[Lancet 391, 679–686; 98% outcome coverage among treated patients, organisational predictors and between-year analyses]{ref}

= Stepped care
{parent=Access to mental health care}

An arrangement offering an appropriate initial level of intervention with planned monitoring and escalation when necessary. Lower-intensity care must not become an indefinite holding pathway for nonresponders or replace immediate specialist assessment where indicated.

= Barrier to mental health care
{parent=Access to mental health care}

A condition that obstructs recognising need, seeking support, entering care or receiving effective intervention. Cost, scheduling, language, service navigation and digital exclusion may operate at different points and require different responses.

= Psychoeducation
{parent=Clinical psychology}

An explanation of a difficulty, its proposed maintaining processes and the rationale for intervention. Understanding can support informed participation, but knowledge gained from an explanation is not itself proof of symptom <Diagnostic remission>[remission].

= Child and adolescent clinical psychology
{parent=Clinical psychology}

The study, assessment and psychological treatment of clinically significant difficulties during childhood and <adolescence>. Developmental understanding, family relationships, school contexts and the young person's perspective affect both assessment and intervention. Evidence from one age range must not be assumed to establish effectiveness across all children and <adolescents>.

= Youth anxiety treatment
{parent=Child and adolescent clinical psychology}

Psychological intervention for <anxiety disorders> in children and <adolescents>. Treatment studies should distinguish <diagnostic remission>, symptom change, global improvement and everyday functioning, as well as immediate outcomes and maintenance. A successful multicomponent intervention establishes an effect of that package under the tested conditions, not the necessity of each included technique.

= Anxiety management
{parent=Youth anxiety treatment}

Strategies intended to recognise, tolerate or regulate <anxiety>, including <coping> routines, relaxation and <breathing exercises>. These should be distinguished from targeted tests of threat predictions. Whether a technique supports approach or becomes a perceived safety requirement depends on its use, and efficacy of a <CBT> package does not isolate the technique's effect.<A meta-analysis to guide the enhancement of CBT for childhood anxiety: exposure over anxiety management by Stephen P. H. Whiteside and others>[Clinical Child and Family Psychology Review 23, 102–121; 75 studies, 111 CBT conditions and component meta-regressions]{ref}

= Emotion identification
{parent=Anxiety management}

Recognising and describing emotional experiences. Performance on an emotion-label task and ability to use that understanding in everyday situations are distinct outcomes.

= Breathing exercise
{parent=Anxiety management}

Deliberate alteration or <attention> to breathing. In an <anxiety> intervention its proposed function should be specified and assessed rather than presuming that any reduction in <Physiological arousal>[arousal] changes the meaning assigned to bodily sensations.

= Relaxation technique
{parent=Anxiety management}

An activity intended to reduce muscular tension or <physiological arousal>. A reduction in immediate <Physiological arousal>[arousal] is a different endpoint from durable change in an <anxiety disorder>.

= Parent involvement in youth anxiety treatment
{parent=Youth anxiety treatment}

Caregivers may facilitate access and practice, receive information, change <Family accommodation>[accommodation], or receive treatment for their own difficulties. These roles imply different mechanisms and should be compared against the same adequate <child> treatment when testing augmentation. Parent-only treatment and adding parent sessions to <child> treatment answer different questions.<A randomized controlled trial of internet-delivered cognitive behaviour therapy for adolescent anxiety disorders in a routine clinical care setting with and without parent sessions by Polly Waite and others>[Child and Adolescent Mental Health 24, 242–250; randomised parent-session allocation, primary remission and follow-up]{ref}

= Guided parent-delivered cognitive behavioral therapy
{parent=Parent involvement in youth anxiety treatment}

= Guided parent-delivered CBT
{synonym}
{c}

A therapist supports a caregiver in delivering structured <CBT> activities with their <child>. Guidance includes reviewing practice and overcoming barriers, rather than merely distributing information. Evidence for a specified guided programme does not establish that an unsupported manual has the same effect.<Treatment of child anxiety disorders via guided parent-delivered cognitive-behavioural therapy by Kerstin Thirlwall and others>[British Journal of Psychiatry 203, 436–444; 194 children, full/brief parent guidance and waiting list; primary-carer eligibility]{ref}

= Supportive Parenting for Anxious Childhood Emotions
{parent=Parent involvement in youth anxiety treatment}
{c}

= SPACE treatment
{synonym}
{c}

A parent-based treatment that targets <Family accommodation>[accommodation] and supportive responses to a <child>'s <anxiety>. A <Randomization>[randomised] trial compared it with <Cognitive behavioral therapy>[child CBT] using prespecified <Noninferiority trial>[noninferiority] criteria; the tested age range and margins constrain the conclusion.<Parent-Based Treatment as Efficacious as Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy for Childhood Anxiety: A Randomized Noninferiority Study of Supportive Parenting for Anxious Childhood Emotions by Eli R. Lebowitz and others>[Journal of the American Academy of Child and Adolescent Psychiatry 59, 362–372; SPACE noninferiority trial, ages 7–14 and accommodation outcomes]{ref}

= Parental anxiety
{parent=Parent involvement in youth anxiety treatment}

<Anxiety> experienced by a parent, distinguished from the <child>'s symptoms and from parental <Family accommodation>[accommodation]. It may affect burden and participation, but association with <child> difficulty does not establish that treating it necessarily augments <child> treatment.<Treatment of childhood anxiety disorder in the context of maternal anxiety disorder: a randomised controlled trial and economic analysis by Cathy Creswell and others>[Health Technology Assessment 19(38); 211-family trial, maternal CBT and mother–child interaction arms]{ref}

= Coping
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Cognitive and behavioral efforts to manage relevant demands or distress. A response that reduces distress immediately can nevertheless maintain a difficulty if it prevents useful <learning> or functioning.

= Coping flexibility
{parent=Coping}

Adjusting <coping> responses to situational demands and available options. A response effective under a controllable short-term challenge may be less useful under prolonged uncontrollable <adversity>.

= Psychological stress
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Responses to demands or threats appraised as taxing or exceeding relevant resources. Stressful experiences may contribute to risk without determining that every exposed person develops a disorder.

= Stress generation
{parent=Psychological stress}

A process in which symptoms or related characteristics increase the probability of subsequent stressful experiences. It differs from sensitization, which concerns a changed response to <psychological stress>, although the two can participate in a feedback loop.

= Psychological stressor
{parent=Psychological stress}

An event or condition placing demands on adaptation, distinguished from appraisal and the resulting psychological or physiological response. Comparable event labels can hide differences in severity, duration and controllability.

= Relapse prevention
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Intervention intended to reduce return of an established disorder or its symptoms after improvement. It is important care, but its evidence should not automatically be reported as prevention of a first-ever disorder.

= Depression
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Depressive mood and related symptoms, or a context-dependent shorthand for a depressive disorder. Research should specify whether the outcome is symptom severity, a current diagnosed episode, first onset or recurrence.

= Cognitive behavioral therapy
{parent=Clinical psychology}

= CBT
{c}
{synonym}

= Cognitive behavioural therapy
{synonym}

A family of psychological treatments using explicit formulations and structured cognitive and behavioral procedures. Treatment tests and modifies relevant <learning>, interpretations and behavior; its content depends on the problem and the evidence rather than on one universal technique.

= Cognitive bias modification
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

Experimental or intervention procedures intended to change biased <information processing>. Demonstrating changed responses on the training task does not automatically demonstrate durable transfer or effective clinical treatment.

= Cognitive behavioral therapy for psychosis
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

= CBT for psychosis
{synonym}
{c}

<Psychological treatment> using an individual <Clinical formulation>[formulation] and collaborative examination of difficulties associated with psychotic experiences. Symptom-specific protocols, general support and usual care provide different comparisons for assessing incremental benefit.

= SlowMo
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy for psychosis}
{c}

A blended digital and face-to-face reasoning intervention for <paranoia>. Its 2021 trial did not establish a significant benefit on the prespecified total <paranoia> score at 24 weeks, despite favourable secondary results. This illustrates why endpoint, timing and process engagement matter when evaluating innovation.<Effects of SlowMo, a Blended Digital Therapy Targeting Reasoning, on Paranoia Among People With Psychosis: A Randomized Clinical Trial by Philippa Garety and others>[JAMA Psychiatry 78, 714–725; 361 in analysis, prespecified 24-week GPTS total endpoint and secondary outcomes]{ref}

= Feeling Safe Programme
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy for psychosis}
{c}

A personalised intervention for persistent <persecutory delusions>, combining modules selected to address relevant maintaining processes. Its <active-control> trial compared it with same-therapist befriending alongside usual care. The continuous conviction result and categorical <Clinical recovery>[recovery] comparison require separate interpretation.<Comparison of a theoretically driven cognitive therapy (the Feeling Safe Programme) with befriending for the treatment of persistent persecutory delusions: a parallel, single-blind, randomised controlled trial by Daniel Freeman and others>[Lancet Psychiatry 8, 696–707; 130 patients, same-therapist befriending, six-month continuous conviction and categorical recovery distinction]{ref}

= Becoming a Man
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}
{c}

A structured youth programme using discussion, rehearsal and examination of automatic interpretations to support better decisions in provocative situations. Randomised school-based evaluations in Chicago tested outcomes including arrests and school engagement; participation-effect estimates and effects of a randomised offer should be distinguished.<Thinking, Fast and Slow? Some Field Experiments to Reduce Crime and Dropout in Chicago by Sara B. Heller and others>[Quarterly Journal of Economics 132, 1–54; two Becoming a Man randomized trials and mechanism analyses]{ref}

= Cognitive restructuring
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

Examining and revising interpretations or assumptions that influence <emotion> and action, typically with explicit alternatives and tests rather than unsupported reassurance. Its contribution to a <treatment> package requires component-level evidence.

= Stress inoculation training
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

Structured <coping>-skills training with practice and application to appropriate challenges. The intervention is distinct from the hypothesis that naturally occurring <adversity> causes later protection; it does not mean deliberately imposing harmful experiences.

= Internet-delivered cognitive behavioral therapy
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

= Internet-delivered CBT
{synonym}
{c}

= Internet CBT
{synonym}
{c}

Delivery of structured <CBT> content through an internet platform, potentially with clinician guidance, parent modules and live contact. Efficacy is a property of the specified programme, population and support arrangement, rather than the delivery medium alone.<Efficacy and cost-effectiveness of therapist-guided internet cognitive behavioural therapy for paediatric anxiety disorders: a single-centre, single-blind, randomised controlled trial by Maral Jolstedt and others>[Lancet Child and Adolescent Health 2, 792–801; 131 children aged 8–12, internet CBT versus child-directed play]{ref}

= Transdiagnostic cognitive behavioral therapy
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

= Transdiagnostic CBT
{synonym}
{c}

An approach addressing processes shared across diagnostic categories. It can still use an individual <clinical formulation> and disorder-relevant behavioural experiments; transdiagnostic delivery need not mean identical exercises for every patient.

= Disorder-specific cognitive behavioral therapy
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

= Disorder-specific CBT
{synonym}
{c}

A protocol organised around mechanisms proposed to maintain a particular disorder. Its comparative value requires an adequate alternative with comparable delivery, competence and assessment; superiority to waiting list alone does not establish superiority to a flexible generic programme.

= Video feedback
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

Use of a recording to compare a concrete prediction about observable behavior with recorded evidence. Preparation matters: selective flaw-searching can reproduce the original bias rather than provide a fair test of the prediction.

= Exposure therapy
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

Planned contact with appropriate feared cues or situations to support new <learning> and reduce maladaptive avoidance. Its rationale requires informed, collaborative tasks and <attention> to the behaviors that could block <learning>; exposure does not mean deliberately creating genuine danger.

= Inhibitory-learning exposure strategy
{parent=Exposure therapy}

A strategy intended to strengthen <learning> that competes with a threat association and improve its later retrieval. Expectancy testing and varied contexts are examples. A coherent <learning> rationale does not itself demonstrate the optimal clinical implementation for every developmental group.<Maximizing exposure therapy: An inhibitory learning approach by Michelle G. Craske and others>[Behaviour Research and Therapy 58, 10–23; theoretical framework and illustrative implementation strategies]{ref}

= One-session treatment of specific phobia
{parent=Exposure therapy}

A concentrated intervention combining approach practice and <learning> about a phobic stimulus. A youth <Randomization>[randomised] trial compared this package with education-support and waiting list. Package efficacy is distinct from proof that one session is the optimal dose for all presentations.<One-session treatment of specific phobias in youth: a randomized clinical trial in the United States and Sweden by Thomas H. Ollendick and others>[Journal of Consulting and Clinical Psychology 77, 504–516; 196 youths, exposure, education-support and waiting-list conditions]{ref}

= Exposure expectancy testing
{parent=Exposure therapy}

Specifying a feared prediction, arranging an informative and appropriately safe approach task, and comparing the observed outcome with the prediction. The review should include uncertainty and alternative explanations rather than compel a reassuring conclusion. Necessary protections and accessibility adjustments are retained.

= Exposure and response prevention
{parent=Exposure therapy}

= ERP
{c}
{synonym}

Exposure to appropriate <obsession>-related triggers while refraining from <compulsions> and functionally equivalent reassurance or neutralization. The target is <learning> to respond differently to the cue and uncertainty, rather than first eliminating every intrusive thought.

= Expectancy violation
{parent=Exposure therapy}

A discrepancy between a predicted outcome and the outcome experienced. During exposure, explicit predictions can make such discrepancies informative; a <safety behavior> may prevent revision if the person attributes the benign outcome to that behavior.

= Interoceptive exposure
{parent=Exposure therapy}

Exposure to appropriate bodily sensations relevant to a feared prediction. Assessment and supervision determine suitable exercises; the inference concerns a specific tested prediction, not the claim that all bodily symptoms are harmless.

= Behavioral experiment
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

A planned test of a specific prediction relevant to a psychological difficulty. The design specifies what would be expected under competing interpretations and reviews the observed outcome, including alternative explanations; it is not simply repeating a feared activity without a <learning> question.

= Cognitive therapy
{parent=Cognitive behavioral therapy}

Treatment examining and modifying interpretations, assumptions and predictions that contribute to distress and impairment. <Behavioral experiments> can generate new evidence rather than relying only on verbal argument or reassurance.

= Threat appraisal
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Interpretation of an event or sensation in terms of its danger, likelihood, consequences and available <coping>. An appraisal becomes a proposed maintaining process when it predicts continuing fear and changes in it alter the relevant response.

= Threat expectancy
{parent=Threat appraisal}

An expectation that a specified adverse outcome will occur under particular conditions. An informative <behavioral experiment> states the prediction and what observed outcome would count against it.

= Intolerance of uncertainty
{parent=Threat appraisal}

A difficulty accepting unresolved possibilities, often accompanied by demands for certainty. It can contribute to checking or <reassurance seeking>, but the function of those responses depends on context and objectively relevant risks.

= Catastrophic misinterpretation
{parent=Threat appraisal}

Interpreting an ambiguous or relatively benign event as evidence of imminent disaster. In cognitive accounts of <panic disorder>, <anxiety>-induced bodily sensations can appear to confirm the threatening interpretation, forming a feedback loop.

= Anxiety
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Apprehension and associated cognitive, bodily and behavioral responses to anticipated threat. <Anxiety> can be adaptive; its presence alone does not imply an <anxiety disorder>.

= Contamination fear
{parent=Anxiety}

<Fear> concerning contact with perceived contaminants or a sense of contamination. Perceived contamination, disgust, danger and compulsive responses are related but distinct measurements.

= Worry
{parent=Anxiety}

Repetitive thought about possible adverse outcomes. It can increase the availability of threat explanations, but occurrence alongside another problem does not by itself establish a causal contribution.

= Health anxiety
{parent=Anxiety}

Concern about having or acquiring illness. Its intensity, interpretation, persistence and effects on functioning vary; <health anxiety> can coexist with physical illness, and concern alone does not establish a disorder.

= Severe health anxiety
{parent=Health anxiety}

Persistent and disabling concern about illness, often involving threatening interpretations, checking, <reassurance seeking> or avoidance. Its <psychological assessment> is distinct from deciding whether a physical illness exists; a <clinical formulation> must allow both to be relevant.

= Anxiety sensitivity
{parent=Anxiety}

Concern that anxiety-related sensations will have harmful consequences. It is conceptually distinct from the current intensity of <anxiety> and from objective interoceptive accuracy.<Heart-beat perception, panic-somatic symptoms and anxiety sensitivity in children by Thalia C. Eley and others>[Behaviour Research and Therapy 42, 439–448; 79 children aged 8–11, mental tracking and symptom/sensitivity measures]{ref}

= Social anxiety
{parent=Anxiety}

<Anxiety> concerning social encounters or evaluation by others. Symptom-selected <social-anxiety> samples should not be described as diagnosed <social anxiety disorder> unless that <Clinical diagnosis>[diagnosis] was assessed.

= Psychological assessment
{parent=Clinical psychology}

Systematic collection and interpretation of information about psychological functioning. Interviews, observations, records and tests provide complementary evidence; a <questionnaire> score does not by itself determine diagnosis, mechanism or treatment.

= Practice effect
{parent=Psychological assessment}

A change in performance resulting from prior exposure to the assessment or related procedures. Repeated measurements can support developmental inference while also changing task familiarity.

= Reading comprehension assessment
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Measurement of understanding under specified text, question, response and timing conditions. An inference about general comprehension requires adequate sampling and evidence that the score is not dominated by an unintended demand.<Reading Comprehension Tests Vary in the Skills They Assess: Differential Dependence on Decoding and Oral Comprehension by Janice M. Keenan and others>[Scientific Studies of Reading 12, 281–300; five measures, differential decoding/listening dependence and developmental changes]{ref}

= Woodcock–Johnson Passage Comprehension subtest
{parent=Reading comprehension assessment}
{c}

= Peabody Individual Achievement Test
{parent=Reading comprehension assessment}
{c}

= PIAT
{synonym}
{c}

= Qualitative Reading Inventory
{parent=Reading comprehension assessment}
{c}

= QRI
{synonym}
{c}

= Gray Oral Reading Test
{parent=Reading comprehension assessment}
{c}

= GORT
{synonym}
{c}

= Cloze test
{parent=Reading comprehension assessment}

A task requiring restoration of omitted text elements. The amount of surrounding context and the response format determine whether local word knowledge, <grammar> or wider discourse integration is needed.

= Clinical caseness
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Meeting a specified <symptom> or diagnostic threshold used to define eligibility or outcome. Crossing a questionnaire threshold and satisfying a diagnostic interview are different operational definitions.

= Routine outcome monitoring
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Repeated measurement of outcomes during ordinary clinical care. It can reveal trajectories, deterioration and lack of progress; an additional design is required to establish that acting on the information improves outcomes.

= Clinical outcome coverage
{parent=Routine outcome monitoring}

The proportion of a clearly specified clinical population with usable outcome measurements. High coverage among treated patients does not establish coverage among all referrals, all people needing care or a common long-term endpoint.

= Outcome feedback
{parent=Routine outcome monitoring}

Providing clinicians or patients with interpretable progress information, sometimes including alerts for trajectories at risk of poor outcome. A trial of feedback linked to supervisory action tests that arrangement, rather than measurement in isolation.<Feedback-informed treatment versus usual psychological treatment for depression and anxiety: a multisite, open-label, cluster randomised controlled trial by Jaime Delgadillo and others>[Lancet Psychiatry 5, 564–572; 77 participating therapists, 2233 patients, cluster allocation and not-on-track outcomes]{ref}

= Not-on-track treatment trajectory
{parent=Outcome feedback}

A trajectory classified by a specified rule as unlikely to reach an expected outcome. An alert should prompt clinical review; its validity, false alarms and feasible responses require evaluation.

= Session-by-session outcome monitoring
{parent=Routine outcome monitoring}

Obtaining outcome measures at successive attended sessions. It can supply a last available score when a planned final contact is missed, while differing endpoint timing and people who never enter treatment remain important limitations.<Improving access to psychological therapy: Initial evaluation of two UK demonstration sites by David M. Clark and others>[Behaviour Research and Therapy 47, 910–920; Doncaster and Newham prospective service cohorts, sessional monitoring and ten-month follow-up]{ref}

= Green et al. Paranoid Thoughts Scale
{parent=Psychological assessment}
{c}

= GPTS
{synonym}
{c}

A <self-report> measure used to assess paranoid thinking, including ideas of reference and persecution. The total score and a persecution subscale should not be substituted for each other after a trial has specified its <primary outcome>.<Effects of SlowMo, a Blended Digital Therapy Targeting Reasoning, on Paranoia Among People With Psychosis: A Randomized Clinical Trial by Philippa Garety and others>[JAMA Psychiatry 78, 714–725; 361 in analysis, prespecified 24-week GPTS total endpoint and secondary outcomes]{ref}

= Psychotic Symptoms Rating Scale
{parent=Psychological assessment}
{c}

= PSYRATS
{synonym}
{c}

A structured clinical rating instrument assessing dimensions of psychotic <symptoms>. A specified delusion-conviction item and an aggregate delusion-severity score are different endpoints.<Comparison of a theoretically driven cognitive therapy (the Feeling Safe Programme) with befriending for the treatment of persistent persecutory delusions: a parallel, single-blind, randomised controlled trial by Daniel Freeman and others>[Lancet Psychiatry 8, 696–707; 130 patients, same-therapist befriending, six-month continuous conviction and categorical recovery distinction]{ref}

= Paranoia
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Suspicious or persecutory thinking, varying in severity and conviction. A dimensional <paranoia> score in a nonclinical sample should not be equated with a <Clinical diagnosis>[diagnosis] or with a persistent <persecutory delusion>.

= Clinical insight
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Recognition or interpretation of one's experiences and their implications for help. <Clinical insight> is multidimensional and variable rather than a complete, all-or-none measure of a person's reasoning or agency.

= Health Anxiety Inventory
{parent=Psychological assessment}
{c}
{title2=HAI}

A <questionnaire> developed to measure <health anxiety> across relevant clinical and medical contexts. A score quantifies aspects of the psychological response to illness-related concerns; it does not determine the presence of physical disease or replace <clinical diagnosis>.<The Health Anxiety Inventory: development and validation of scales for the measurement of health anxiety and hypochondriasis by Paul Salkovskis and others>[2002; original clinical contrasts, sensitivity-to-change studies and medical samples]{ref}

= Strange situation procedure
{parent=Psychological assessment}

A structured observational procedure using episodes of caregiver presence, separation and reunion to assess attachment-related behavior. Interpretation concerns the organized pattern of behavior in context rather than assuming that either distress or absence of distress alone establishes security.

= Clinical diagnosis
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Classification of a presentation using specified diagnostic criteria. A <Clinical diagnosis>[diagnosis] groups observed features; it does not, by itself, identify the causal processes maintaining a particular person's difficulties.

= Core needle biopsy
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Liver function test
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Wood's lamp
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}
{c}

An examination lamp used as an adjunct in some <skin> assessments; its appearance is interpreted with the clinical findings.

= Heart murmur
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Neurological sign
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Echocardiography
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Endoscopy
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Pregnancy test
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Blood test
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Blood count
{parent=Blood test}

= Percussion
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Auscultation
{parent=Clinical diagnosis}

= Stethoscope
{parent=Auscultation}

= Comorbidity
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Co-occurrence of more than one disorder or clinically relevant condition. Selecting one <principal diagnosis> does not make additional difficulties irrelevant to formulation, outcomes or access.

= Principal diagnosis
{parent=Psychological assessment}

The <Clinical diagnosis>[diagnosis] designated primary under a stated assessment rule. Losing the <principal diagnosis> does not necessarily mean losing all comorbid disorders.

= Qualitative interview
{parent=Psychological assessment}

A structured or flexible interview used to investigate experiences, meanings and processes. It can identify candidate barriers and explain implementation without by itself estimating their population prevalence or the <causal effect> of an intervention.

= Behavioral approach task
{parent=Psychological assessment}

= Behavioral approach test
{synonym}

A structured opportunity to approach a feared stimulus or situation, with behavioural performance and <fear> measured according to a specified protocol. Novel-context tasks can assess transfer; willingness, safety constraints and task demands still affect the result.

= Somatic symptom
{parent=Psychological assessment}

A bodily symptom, without presupposing its cause. Describing a symptom as somatic does not establish whether it arises from medical, psychological or interacting processes.

= Clinical Global Impression scale
{parent=Psychological assessment}
{c}

= CGI
{synonym}
{c}

Clinician-rated global severity and improvement measures. A rating of much or very much improved on the improvement scale is a response endpoint, not automatically freedom from the original <Clinical diagnosis>[diagnosis].<Cognitive behavioral therapy, sertraline, or a combination in childhood anxiety by John T. Walkup and others>[New England Journal of Medicine 359, 2753–2766; CAMS, Table 2 and twelve-week CGI-I response]{ref}

= Clinical Global Impression–Improvement scale
{parent=Clinical Global Impression scale}
{c}

= CGI-I
{synonym}
{c}

= Functional impairment
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Interference with everyday activities, relationships or participation. Symptom reduction can occur without full restoration of functioning, so both outcomes are informative.

= Performance test
{parent=Psychological assessment}

A task scored by observed responses under specified conditions. Choosing task content, response format and a scoring criterion determines which abilities and method demands can influence the score.

= Self-report
{parent=Psychological assessment}

Information a person provides about their own experiences, characteristics or behavior. Response style, self-knowledge and interpretation affect scores; self-perceived ability is not equivalent to demonstrated ability.

= Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale
{parent=Psychological assessment}
{c}
{title2=Y-BOCS}

A clinician-rated measure of obsessive-compulsive symptom severity used to monitor outcomes. Its interpretation depends on assessment and the trial's specified response or remission criterion; a symptom score is not identical to functioning or quality of life.

= Clinical formulation
{parent=Psychological assessment}

A testable account linking a person's difficulties to relevant precipitating, maintaining and protective processes. A formulation guides intervention and should be revised when predicted effects do not occur; it is more informative than merely repeating the diagnosis.

= Need for care
{parent=Clinical formulation}

A requirement for support or treatment assessed through difficulties, distress, impairment, risk, context and preferences. Actual service contact is an imperfect proxy because access, willingness and <discrimination> affect who receives care.

= Treatment component
{parent=Clinical formulation}

A distinguishable element of an <intervention>, such as exposure, attentional training or organizing skills. Evidence that a package works is different from evidence that one component is necessary or sufficient.

= Transdiagnostic process
{parent=Clinical formulation}

A process relevant across more than one diagnostic category. A shared <safety behavior> or attentional process can justify common <intervention> principles while its feared meaning and context differ between individuals.

= Attributional style
{parent=Psychology}

A tendency to explain outcomes using particular kinds of causes. The helplessness reformulation distinguishes internal from external, stable from unstable, and global from specific explanations to address self-esteem, persistence and generalization.

= Affective processing bias
{parent=Psychology}

A systematic difference in processing emotional information, such as preferential recognition or later recall of negative over positive material. A change in this measure may precede a change in subjective mood.

= Depressed mood
{parent=Psychology}

A sustained or recurring low affective state. It is one component of <major depressive disorder>, not by itself equivalent to that diagnosis.

= Neuropsychology
{parent=Psychology}

Study of relations between <brain> function and cognition or behaviour, including patterns of impairment and preservation after <brain lesions>.

= Neurological research participant
{parent=Neuropsychology}

A person whose participation in research on neurological function provides evidence about cognition or <brain> mechanisms. Case studies may be published under initials or patient codes.

= List of neurological research participants
{parent=Neurological research participant}

= Patient K. F.
{parent=List of neurological research participants}
{c}

The published patient code in classic investigations of severe auditory-verbal short-term impairment with relatively preserved performance on several long-term verbal-learning tasks. The published initials are retained here.

= Henry Molaison
{parent=List of neurological research participants}
{c}
{wiki}

The research participant originally reported as H.M. Studies after bilateral <medial temporal lobe> surgery provided evidence for distinctions among immediate retention, new declarative learning and skill learning.

= Amnesia
{parent=Neuropsychology}

A pathological impairment of memory. Specify the affected time period, task and process rather than assuming loss of all forms of learning or all prior knowledge.

= Diencephalic amnesia
{parent=Amnesia}

Memory impairment associated with diencephalic damage, including thalamic or mammillary pathways. Connected cortical dysfunction and accompanying pathology complicate precise localization.

= Korsakoff syndrome
{parent=Diencephalic amnesia}
{c}

A severe amnesic syndrome associated with thiamine deficiency, often but not exclusively in the context of alcohol misuse. Its eponym refers to <Sergei Korsakoff>. Co-occurring damage varies across patients, making comparison with non-amnesic pathological controls valuable.

= Retrograde amnesia
{parent=Amnesia}

Loss or impaired retrieval of information acquired before onset of the condition. Its temporal extent and detail can differ from deficits in new learning.

= Anterograde amnesia
{parent=Amnesia}

Impaired formation or retention of new memories after onset of the condition, distinct from a general loss of sensory perception or intelligence.

= Double dissociation
{parent=Neuropsychology}

Two interventions or patient groups show opposite relative impairments on two tasks. This constrains a simple account based only on general task difficulty, but does not prove that the underlying <neural circuits> are independent: task sensitivity, lesion extent and alternative strategies still matter.

= Crossed performance rejects a single monotone resource
{parent=Double dissociation}

Suppose performances on fixed tasks $A$ and $B$ are $f_A(r)$ and $f_B(r)$, where both <functions> are <Nondecreasing function>[nondecreasing] in the same <scalar> resource $r$. If patient $P$ performs strictly better than $Q$ on $A$, then $r_P>r_Q$: the reverse ordering would contradict monotonicity. If $Q$ performs strictly better on $B$, the same argument gives $r_Q>r_P$, a contradiction. Thus a genuine crossover rejects this single-resource model.

The conclusion depends on the fixed mappings and task comparability. It does not prove isolated anatomical stores, and it need not apply when patients differ in strategy, sensory access or other task parameters.

